FILE: vUX-component.js
FEATURE: component-module
DATE: 2026-09-17
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'component-teardown'

REASON
NEW FILE: vUX-component.js

The public surface: Component (the definition) and Instance (one mounted node).

Shape decisions:

  - The constructor takes an ELEMENT, not a name. An earlier draft took a name and
    looked up 'template[data-v-component=...]'. Taking the element deletes the
    registry outright, and with it name collisions, module-level global state and
    three error cases. It also makes instance.mount(childComponent, ...) type-check
    instead of failing at runtime on a mistyped name.

  - Either a <template> or ordinary in-place markup is accepted. The second form
    renders as a visible placeholder until initialize() lifts it, and is what makes
    the DataView refactor fit: DataView's row template is a plain element.

  - The template is resolved in initialize(), not in the constructor, so markup
    added to the DOM after construction still works and an in-place template is not
    torn out of the page before the consumer asks for it.

  - instance.data returns a COPY. Handing back the live object invites a consumer
    to mutate it, which would put the bindings out of step with the data they were
    last rendered from, with no way for the instance to notice.

  - update() merges rather than replaces, and returns the list of fields that
    actually moved, so a caller can tell a real change from a no-op write.

  - A child mounted through instance.mount() is owned by the parent and destroyed
    with it. Children are destroyed BEFORE the parent's onDestroy fires, so a
    child's own onDestroy still sees its parent's node in place.

  - sync() refuses a second container. The reconcile owns every child of the
    container it manages; two components sharing one container would each treat the
    other's nodes as rows that had disappeared, and would silently delete them.


